@ Index:   Tide predictions: the high and low waters and the tide curve of a day, by harmonic constants
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.3.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/tide47
@ Tested:  C47 simulator (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_tide47.py, the astronomy against NOAA's tables (XTide),
@ Tested:  the constants back from a year of predictions (UTide), the C47 against python/tide47ref.py (25 ports x 3
@ Tested:  days, to 0.01 s and 1E-12 m); not yet run on a calculator (LN and LBL? are new there)
@ Input:   the data programs loaded (TIDEME, TIDEUS, TIDELA: any of them); the day from the calculator's date
@ Output:  Mina Salman (Bahrain) 2026-10-25: the day's curve and the table of its high and low waters (LAT)
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Tide 47 predicts the tide on the C47 / R47: for any day at a port, the times and heights of the high and low waters
@ and the tide curve of the day, worked out on the calculator by the harmonic method that NOAA's and the Admiralty's
@ tables use (60 constituents with the 18.6-year nodal corrections, as whole matrices). The ports come in data
@ programs by region: TIDEME (the Middle East: Bahrain, Oman, Iran, Pakistan, Yemen, Djibouti), TIDEUS (ten US ports)
@ and TIDELA (Latin America). Heights are in metres above the chart datum (LAT, or MLLW for the US ports).
@ NOT FOR NAVIGATION: the astronomical tide only (wind, pressure and surges add to it); the official tables of the
@ port authority are the reference.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load TIDE47 and the data programs of the regions you want (one is enough: TIDE47 finds those loaded), then press
@ XEQ TIDE47. Setting up takes about 8 seconds; it shows today at the first port. The day is the calculator's date;
@ the dotted line on the curve is its clock (set the calculator to the port's local time).
@   4 6   the day before / after            1 3   the week before / after          5   today
@   8 2   the port before / after           +     the next region loaded           0   the end
@ The screen: the port, the date, its time zone, the datum and the source; the day's curve (hours 00-24) with each
@ high and low water marked; the table of the high and low waters with their heights and ranges. A new day or port
@ takes about 3.5 seconds. A data program from an older version is left out with OLD DATA, REBUILD.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack and LASTx are given back at the end. Registers R00-R19 are not touched; R20-R99 are used and cleared at
@ the end. It makes its matrices while it runs (DM UM EM SPD W LW ONES AV BV LF HG HH G LH P L A AW AW2 Z HS) and
@ deletes them at the end. The angle mode is left in DEG; GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 are set.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ TIDE47 is one program with one global label (TIDE47); every routine is a local label. Each data program is one
@ program with one global label (TIDEME, TIDEUS, TIDELA) and a local label per port. Written with the STRUCT commands,
@ the matrix functions, ATEXT and GRFNT of the C47 / R47 firmware (00.109.05.00a0.ALPHA of 5 Oct 2026 or later); LN
@ and LBL? are used here for the first time on a calculator (see the README).
@
@ ---- Technical notes (from the commented source) ----
@ TIDE47 v0.3.0 - Tide 47: tide and tidal-stream predictions for the C47 / R47 (and the DM42n with the C47 firmware)
@ The harmonic method of NOAA and the Admiralty (Schureman, US C&GS Special Publication 98):
@ h(t) = Z0 + sum f H cos(V + u - g + w t)
@ over 60 constituents (NOAA's 37, 8 of TICON's, 15 for the Aramco tables), with the 18.6-year nodal corrections f and u, as whole
@ matrices (the firmware works on every element: SIN COS LN e^x, M + M, M - M, M x r, M MOD r):
@ P = DM x AV + UM x BV - G  (MOD 360)     the phases at the day's start: DM the Doodson numbers (60 x 7), AV
@ the angles tau s h p N' p1 90; UM the u combinations, BV xi nu nu'
@ 2nu'' Q R p
@ A = e^(EM x LF + LH)                     the amplitudes f H: EM the node-factor powers (60 x 10), LF the logs of
@ the 10 basic factors, LH the logs of the port's H (no loop for f x H)
@ h(t) = Z0 + A . COS(W t + P)            one height: one COS of a 60-vector and one DOT
@ High and low waters: the hourly signs of h'(t) = -AW . SIN(W t + P), then Newton (h'' = -AW2 . COS) in the
@ hour. python/tide47ref.py is the same, step for step (tests/test_tide47.py compares them).
@ The ports are in the data programs TIDEME, TIDEUS, TIDELA, TIDECU (load those you want: TIDE47 finds them, LBL?):
@ XEQ "TIDEME" with X = the port's number sets its name (R26), time zone (R27), the kind (R28: + 1 = US daylight
@ time; + 2 a rotary stream, + 4 a rectilinear stream), datum (R29; a stream: its units and directions), source
@ (R95), Z0 (R24) and the matrices HG (HV); X = 0: R96 the number of ports, R97 the region, R93 the data version.
@ Tidal streams (R28 >= 2): HG the constants of the speed along the flood direction (rectilinear, knots, + flood,
@ - ebb; the variable SDIR = the flood direction + the ebb's / 1000) or of the east component u (rotary: then HV
@ the north component v, SDIR its mean V0). The events are the turning points of the speed: g = u u' + v v'
@ (= s s') changes sign: the maxima (flood / ebb, MAX) and the minima (slack, MIN), with the set (the direction the
@ stream goes, deg true). tide47ref.sday is the twin.
@ Keys: 4 6 the day before / after, 1 3 the week before / after, 5 today, 8 2 the port before / after, + the next
@ region loaded (MIDDLE EAST, USA, LATIN AMERICA, US STREAMS), 0 the end.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = the bottom: the header at 220 and 210, the chart at x 26-386 (15 pixels an hour), rows
@ 118-196 (the time axis at 115), the table rows 0-100.
@ Registers: R20 the region, R21 the port, R22 the day's start (JD, UT), R23 the time zone of the day (DST in),
@ R24 Z0, R25 the day (JDN of the local date), R26-R29 R95-R97 the port, R30-R39 R50-R59 the astronomy, R40-R47
@ the calendar, R50-R58 the events' search, R60-R65 the event times (hours), R66-R71 their heights, R72-R77 their
@ kinds (1 high, -1 low), R78 how many (a stream: the matrix EV, a row an event: time, value, set, kind), R79 the
@ data programs found (LBL :PROBE:), R80 R81 the chart's lowest and highest heights, R82 its step, R83 pixels a
@ metre, R84 R87-R91 R93 drawing, R85 today (JDN), R86 the hour now, R92 1: load the port, R98 the key.
@ The user's stack and LASTx are kept (LocR 9: STOS R.00, LASTx in R.08); R00-R19 are not touched; at the end
@ R20-R99 are cleared and the matrices deleted; the angle mode is left in DEG.
@

LBL "TIDE47"
LocR 9
STOS R.00
LASTx
STO R.08
XEQ 01                          ; -> :INIT:
@ the ports: only when a data program was found (R79, LBL :PROBE:)
RCL 79
X≠0?
IF 01
  1
  STO 92
  DO 01
    RCL 92
    X≠0?
    XEQ 07                      ; -> :LOAD:
    XEQ 11                      ; -> :DAY:
    XEQ 47                      ; -> :KEYS:
    82
    RCL 98
    X≠Y?
  WHILE 01
  ENDDO 01
ENDIF 01
XEQ 00                          ; -> :END:
RCL R.08
STO L
RCLS R.00
RTN
@
@ ---- LBL :END: R20-R99 cleared (R-CLR 20.79, then R99), the matrices deleted, GRMOD 0, GRFNT 20, the screen
LBL 00                          ; :END:
20.79
R-CLR
0
STO 99
DELITM "DM"
DELITM "UM"
DELITM "EM"
DELITM "SPD"
DELITM "W"
DELITM "LW"
DELITM "ONES"
DELITM "AV"
DELITM "BV"
DELITM "LF"
DELITM "HG"
DELITM "HH"
